    11	modular. v4 applies the async round-3 and round-4 reviews (HavocFG with
    12	anchor-value conditions, row inclusion, finality head): anchors as a function of the
    13	duty view with the F basis; Static and TargetPreserving replace
    14	LeftAlone; the quiet window is replaced by the certified-pair rule; NJ
    15	step with two arms; honest sourcing is local. E7 is withdrawn in favour
    16	of a parametric composite (HavocFG).
    17	Per-item agreement status is in §11._
    18	
    19	## 0. Purpose
    20	
    21	The current repository proves one fixed bundle (`AvailableChain`,
    22	Draft 4.3) per protocol. This design replaces the bundle with a framework
    23	that stays stable while the protocol search changes direction.
    24	
    25	```text
    26	┌────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    27	│ G1 │ Protocol-agnostic. The engine, conditions and claims do not       │
    28	│    │ assume Goldfish, votes per slot, a state shape, or a leader.      │
    29	│ G2 │ Additive evolution. A new assumption, claim, output kind or key   │
    30	│    │ scheme is an addition. Existing certificates stay valid.          │
    31	│ G3 │ Partial certification. An instance certifies any subset of the    │
    32	│    │ claims. Each cell is proved, refuted (checked witness) or open.   │
    33	│ G4 │ Mechanical comparison. Dominance between instances is computed   │
    34	│    │ from the certificates, not argued.                                │
    35	│ G5 │ Composition-ready. The FG is one fixed, verified module (our      │
    36	│    │ Simplex FG). An available protocol (AP) plugs into it through a   │
    37	│    │ small interface and gets the composite claims by proving AP-side  │
    38	│    │ catalog entries only.                                             │
    39	│ G6 │ Cheap falsification. The same definitions run in a bounded        │
    40	│    │ explorer; a counterexample becomes a refutation certificate.     │
    41	└────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
    42	```
    43	
    44	Non-goals: probability inside Lean (finite-committee error stays a
    45	deterministic premise, with the probability bound outside Lean); one
    46	generic proof for all protocols; porting verified-consensus.
    47	
    48	Design test: for each engine feature, ask "can a later need be met by an
    49	addition in L1–L3, or does it force an engine version?" Features that
    50	would force an engine version are added to v2 now (E1, E2, E5, E6).
    51	Features that can be added later as conditions are not (weights, ε).
    52	
    53	## 1. Layers
    54	
    55	```text
    56	┌────┬──────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬────────────────────┐
    57	│ L  │ Layer        │ Content                       │ Change policy      │
    58	├────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┤
    59	│ L0 │ Engine       │ events, world, run, emission  │ versioned; a new   │
    60	│    │              │                               │ version comes with │
    61	│    │              │                               │ a lift theorem     │
    62	│ L1 │ Conditions   │ named run predicates;         │ append-only;       │
    63	│    │              │ regimes = records of them     │ immutable once     │
    64	│    │              │                               │ referenced         │
    65	│ L2 │ Facets       │ what an instance exposes:     │ append-only        │
    66	│    │              │ outputs, proposals, protected │                    │
    67	│    │              │ sets, FG domain, evidence     │                    │
    68	│ L3 │ Catalog      │ claim specs with constants,   │ append-only;       │
    69	│    │              │ order, monotonicity           │ immutable ids      │
    70	│ L4 │ Instances    │ protocol + facets +           │ free               │
    71	│    │              │ certificates and refutations  │                    │
    72	│ L5 │ Kits         │ optional proof libraries      │ free; never        │
    73	│    │              │ (engine lemmas, Goldfish, FG, │ imported by L0–L3  │
    74	│    │              │ composition)                  │                    │
    75	│ L6 │ Registry     │ generated matrix; explorer    │ generated          │
    76	└────┴──────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴────────────────────┘
    77	```
    78	
    79	Rule: L0–L3 import nothing from L4–L5. An instance never changes L0–L3.
    80	A hole found in L1–L3 is fixed by a new versioned entry and, when useful,
    81	a relation lemma to the old entry (implies, equivalent, or refutes).
    82	Example: a nested strong output (the deepest PC-certified prefix of S)
    83	needs its own entry with arming lag 4Δ (r134 §6.2–6.3); the raw strong
    84	entry and its SA-PC certificate stay as they are (§11, H1).
    85	
    86	## 2. Engine v2 (L0)
    87	
    88	The engine keeps the current semantics (`World.step`; emission changes
    89	state only through E5; asleep nodes emit nothing; integer time; static
    90	honest set). It removes protocol assumptions that would otherwise force
    91	later engine versions.
    92	
    93	**E1. Duties and keys.** `Auth` becomes `seat (ℓ : Label) (i : Fin (seats ℓ))`
    94	or `none`. The proposer is not a special key: a proposal duty is a label
    95	with one seat. `ProtocolSpec` drops `propose`, `proposalTime`, `slotsAt`
    96	and `Run.proposerOf`; `act st ℓ` produces the payload of every duty.
    97	Reason: proposer committees, inclusion-list committees (FOCIL), VRF
    98	buckets with growing eligibility and multi-proposer designs are then
    99	labels, not engine changes. Claims that need a proposal read the
   100	`Proposals` facet (§3): the label→time map, "seat 0 of `prop s` is
   101	honest and awake", and a proposal identity read from the payload, not
   102	from the key. (Async review, Q1: no blocker.)
   103	
   104	**E2. Per-label seat counts; arbitrary assignment.** `seats : Label → Nat`
   105	and `Run.seatOf ℓ : Fin (seats ℓ) → V` (a function, not a bijection).
   106	The committee model moves to L1 as conditions:
   107	
   108	```text
   109	┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   110	│ Condition        │ Meaning                                          │
   111	├──────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   112	│ CStar F          │ for labels in family F: seats = |V| and seatOf   │
   113	│                  │ is a bijection (today's C*)                      │
   114	│ Linked F         │ for labels in family F: one fixed bijection for  │
   115	│                  │ all labels (validator identity; FFG, RLMD-GHOST) │
   116	│ SingleSeat F     │ proposal duties: one seat, holder = run data     │
   117	│ Weighted w F     │ (later) validator v holds w v seats of each      │
   118	│                  │ label in F                                       │
   119	│ Balanced ε F     │ (later) finite committees: fractions within ε    │
   120	└──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   121	```
   122	
   123	Honest code still never sees keys (K2 unchanged). Under `Linked`, a
   124	receiver links two objects through equal seat indices; the sender needs
   125	no identity. Reusing seat indices as identities across C* labels stays
   126	refuted (fg-sa-scope.md, r94); `Linked` is a different condition.
   127	
   128	**E3. Weights: not in the engine.** Stake weight is seat multiplicity
   129	(`Weighted`), and weighted participation premises are new L1 conditions
   130	with a weight parameter. No engine field is needed, so weights are
   131	deferred without risk. (Async review, Q6: defer.)
   132	
   133	**E4. Hook obligations.** Network conditions read protocol hooks
   134	(`excludes`, `processed`, `handles`, `depsPresent`, `carries`). An
   135	instance could weaken a premise through a hook. Every certificate
   136	includes `HooksSound P`:
   137	- `excludes` is stable under `clock`, `process` and `recordOwn`, and
   138	  implies that `process` leaves the state unchanged;
   139	- `processed` is monotone. It need not mean "the state holds the object":
   140	  SA-PC's `processed(pc)` holds once all witnesses are received, even if
   141	  the PC object was never delivered. That is sound and allowed;
   142	- `depsPresent` is monotone (otherwise an instance can postpone its
   143	  delivery obligations forever);
   144	- `handles o o'` implies `o = o' ∨ carries o o'`.
   145	
   146	Checked 2026-09-28 by both sessions: Grid CO, SA, SA-PC and L* satisfy
   147	all four (`excludes := false`; `receivedHeads`, `blockKnown`, `pathKnown`
   148	only grow; SA-PC `pcValid` is monotone; handler lists are sublists of the
   149	carries lists: SA-PC Handlers.lean:95, Grid CO Protocol.lean:364).
   150	
   151	Phase 1 check: SA-PC `process` ignores a PC before `pcEnvelopeReady`
   152	(slot+1 ≤ current slot) with `excludes = false`. Confirm that no proof
   153	assumes one delivery suffices for acceptance; eventual acceptance comes
   154	from the derived `processed` or from relay.
   155	
   156	Hook obligations alone do not stop vacuity. A spec with `processed`
   157	always true, or `depsPresent` always false, admits no delivery under
   158	`DeliveryWellFormed`; every delivery-based regime is then empty for that
   159	instance and every claim holds vacuously. Today's regime witnesses use toy
   160	protocols, not the instance. Therefore every certificate also carries an
   161	**instance regime witness** (§5). (Async review: agreed.)
   162	
   163	**E5. Own-signature record (prerequisite for FG).** FG duties need a
   164	durable record of the validator's own signatures: the DC validator record
   165	Λ (targets, timeouts, locks) is updated from the node's own attestations.
   166	Under K3 this cannot be written; FG-SA cannot be written on the v1
   167	engine. Engine v2 adds:
   168	
   169	- `selfVisible : Label → Bool` and `recordOwn : State → Object → State`.
   170	  After an awake tick, the node applies `recordOwn` to each object it
   171	  emitted for a self-visible label. `recordOwn` is identity by default.
   172	- **C\* separation.** Certificates of claims whose regime uses `CStar F`
   173	  prove `selfVisible ℓ = false` for every ℓ in F. Feedback exists only
   174	  for full-set duties, where it reveals no seat.
   175	- **Record isolation** (async review). The protocol supplies a projection
   176	  `base : State → B` such that `base` after `clock`, `process` and
   177	  `recordOwn` is a function of `base` before (and of the time or object),
   178	  and `recordOwn` does not change `base`. The `act` of every C* label and
   179	  every output getter factor through `base`. So the awake pattern at FG
   180	  ticks cannot leak into the base state, and the base proofs keep their
   181	  "same receipts ⇒ same view" arguments. In DC, `get_fg_root` reads the
   182	  chain state, not Λ; only the voter's own attestation reads Λ.
   183	
   184	**E6. Restart events.** `Event` gains `restart (v : V) (t : Time)`:
   185	`st ↦ P.restore (P.durable st)`. The durable part is the E5 record; the
   186	base part resets to `init`. The record must survive: DC accountable
   187	safety and "honest never slashed" rely on an honest validator never
   188	signing conflicting FG votes, and the node knows that only from Λ (real
   189	clients persist slashing protection). Record isolation makes the split
   190	clean: base resets, record persists. A restarted node's base starts from
   191	genesis, so base claims treat it as a joiner until a rejoin condition
   192	holds. Today
   193	every honest node ticks at every public time and never loses state, so
   194	no claim covers late joiners or restarts (async review, Q8 (3); r145
   195	lists both as open). An event constructor cannot be added later without
   196	an engine version, so it is added now. Existing regimes add the
   197	condition `NoRestart`. State transfer to a joiner is a protocol payload,
   198	not an engine feature. Relay stays a run premise (`AcceptedObjectRelay`);
   199	a "protocol relay only" condition, in which only emissions and protocol
   200	gossip are delivered, is a later L1 addition.
   201	
   202	**E7 (withdrawn in v4).** v3 proposed run inputs for the FG anchor set.
   203	The anchors are a fixed function of the duty view (§7.2), so AP-side
   204	entries are stated for `AP ⊗ HavocFG` instead, with no engine change.
   205	
   206	**Lift theorem.** `ofV1 : ProtocolSpecV1 n → ProtocolSpec` maps today's
   207	specs (every committee label gets `n` seats; each slot gets a one-seat
   208	proposal label; no self-visible labels; `durable` is trivial). The theorem maps every v2 run
   209	under `CStar ∧ SingleSeat ∧ NoRestart` to a v1 run with the same reads,
   210	and back. Consequence: all existing instance proofs transfer without
   211	re-proof, including the 69 `proposerOf` uses in Grid CO and 80 in SA-PC.
   212	Every later engine change uses the same pattern.
   213	
   214	## 3. Facets (L2)
   215	
   216	A facet is a small structure over a `ProtocolSpec`. A claim names the
   217	facets it reads; an instance supplies them.
   218	
   219	```text
   220	┌──────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   221	│ Facet        │ Content                                              │
   222	├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   223	│ ChainOutputs │ named outputs `o : State → Block` (confirmed, stable,│
   224	│              │ strong, finalized, …)                                │
   225	│ Proposals    │ proposal labels `prop : Slot → Label`, label times,  │
   226	│              │ `block : Payload → Option Block`, `opening`          │
   227	│ Protected    │ `protected : State → Block → Bool`: the base's       │
   228	│              │ internal protected set (for SA: live leases ∪ S)     │
   229	│ FGTarget     │ `target : State → Option (Block × Cert)`: the AP's   │
   230	│              │ certified FG target, read at the FG action time      │
   231	│              │ (§7.2)                                               │
   232	│ AnchorChoice │ the AP's rule for picking one anchor from            │
   233	│              │ `anchorsOf` of a duty view; read only by the HEAD    │
   234	│              │ and proposal walks (§7.2)                            │
   235	│ Base         │ the record-isolation projection of E5                │
   236	└──────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   237	```
   238	
   239	Every output getter reads only the `base` projection (E5). This covers
   240	`finalized` too: DC `get_fg_root` reads chain state, not Λ.
   241	
   242	Claims are schemas over output names. `Safe o`, `Monotone o`,
   243	`Included o`, `Persists o`, `Prefix o₁ o₂` work for any output, so a new
   244	output needs no new claim code. A single-ledger family maps `stable` and
   245	`confirmed` to one getter; no equality claim is needed.
   246	
   247	The FG module's own interface (certificates, evidence, attributable
   248	signers under `Linked`, with extraction and verification theorems; r94)
   249	is internal to `Kit.FG` and is not a facet: the FG is fixed.
   250	
   251	Facets that expose internal state (`Protected`, `FGTarget`) are
   252	protocol-defined. This is sound: a composition theorem uses the same
   253	facet on both sides, so a small or trivial `Protected` only makes the
   254	anchored claims harder to prove, never false.
   255	
   256	## 4. Claim catalog (L3)
   257	
   258	```lean
   259	structure ClaimSpec where
   260	  id       : Lean.Name                 -- immutable, e.g. `ac.safe.v1`
   261	  Facets   : {n : Nat} → ProtocolSpec n → Type
   262	  Prem     : Type                      -- premise parameters (θrec, faultCap,
   263	                                       -- recoveryFull, Π, q, …)
   264	  Concl    : Type                      -- conclusion parameters (D, ρ, A, …)
   265	  valid    : Prem → Concl → Prop
   266	  weakerPrem   : Prem → Prem → Prop    -- preorder: p ≤ p' = p admits more runs
   267	  strongerConcl : Concl → Concl → Prop -- preorder: c ≤ c' = c promises more
   268	  holds    : {V : Type} → [Fintype V] → [DecidableEq V] →
   269	             {n : Nat} → (P : ProtocolSpec n) → Facets P → Prem → Concl → Prop
   270	  monoPrem  : weakerPrem p p' → holds P F p c → holds P F p' c
   271	  monoConcl : strongerConcl c c' → holds P F p c → holds P F p c'
   272	```
   273	
   274	The user's rule is that tradeoffs are reported constants. Premise
   275	parameters (SA recovery at h>a versus SA-PC at a≤f with q ≤ awake
   276	honest; θrec; recoveryFull; faultCap; Π) are ordered opposite to
   277	conclusion parameters: a weaker premise is a stronger claim. With one
   278	order for both, dominance would be wrong.
   279	
   280	- `holds` quantifies over every environment and run of its regime.
   281	  Regimes come only from L1; an instance cannot add a premise.
   282	- Every regime is prefix-closed or states that it is not. The anchored
   283	  composition (§7) needs prefix-closed regimes.
   284	- Constants belong to one claim. A cross-claim bound (`Π ≤ η`,
   285	  `A_f ≤ A`) goes into a claim that reads both constants.
   286	- `monoPrem` and `monoConcl` are proved once per claim. With them,
   287	  dominance is computable: instance X dominates Y on a claim set S when
   288	  X certifies every claim of S with premises at least as weak and
   289	  conclusions at least as strong.
   290	- Relation lemmas (`implies`, `equivalent`) between catalog entries are
   291	  proved once. Derived certificates follow without instance work.
   292	
   293	Every catalog entry also needs:
   294	- a discriminating witness: a trivial instance from the cheater set
   295	  (`alwaysGenesis`, echo-proposal, and so on) that fails it. This shows
   296	  that the claim is not vacuous;
   297	- a regime witness at catalog level, as today (K8).
   298	
   299	**Profiles.** A profile is a named set of catalog ids. `ac-4.3` is the
   300	current bundle. A proved equivalence `AvailableChain ↔ ac-4.3` feeds
   301	today's theorems (`gridContract_from_engine`, `saContract_from_engine`,
   302	`sapcContract_from_engine`) into the matrix unchanged. `StrongClaims`
   303	becomes profile `strong-1` the same way. The user decides which profile
   304	is the required floor for a search task.
   305	
   306	`RecoveredAt` is split: each Sleepy claim gets a recovered variant
   307	(`ac.recovered.safe.v1`, …), because FG-SA joint recovery needs them
   308	separately (async review, Q5). `ac-4.3` contains all four, so the
   309	equivalence still holds.
   310	
   311	Proposed tiers:
   312	
   313	```text
   314	┌──────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   315	│ Tier         │ Entries                                               │
   316	├──────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   317	│ core         │ safe(confirmed), safe(stable), monotone, prefix       │
   318	│              │ (stable ⪯ confirmed), included(confirmed)             │
   319	│ standard     │ included(stable) under StableSleepy, fast inclusion,  │
   320	│              │ persistence (general and fast), recovered variants    │
   321	│ extra        │ strong output claims; base exports for FG:            │
   322	│              │ `base.coherent` (all live honest leases and all S lie │
   323	│              │ on one chain, for all a<h), `base.sourceTransfer`     │
   324	│              │ (under synchrony a fast source is in every honest     │
   325	│              │ HEAD from +5Δ and stays; r106 item 2c);               │
   326	│              │ P1 from a recovered handoff (repeated outages);       │
   327	│              │ anything added later                                  │
   328	│ fg-ready     │ AP-side entries (§7.3): `ap.floored`,                 │
   329	│ (AP side)    │ `ap.anchorCompliant`, `ap.honestSourced`,             │
   330	│              │ `ap.targetSafe`, `ap.targetLive`,                     │
   331	│              │ `ap.cleanCompatible`, `ap.includesRows`               │
   332	│ composite    │ derived by `Kit.Compose` for AP ⊗ SimplexFG (§7.5):   │
   333	│              │ accountable safety, honest never slashable, finalized │
   334	│              │ agreement under the cap, finalized monotone,          │
   335	│              │ finalized ⪯ available, the AP's claims in the         │
   336	│              │ composite, joint recovery, finalized inclusion and    │
   337	│              │ growth after recovery                                 │
   338	└──────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   339	```
   340	
   341	## 5. Certificates, refutations, registry (L4, L6)
   342	
   343	```lean
   344	structure Cert (c : ClaimSpec) (P : ProtocolSpec n) where
   345	  facets    : c.Facets P
   346	  prem      : c.Prem
   347	  concl     : c.Concl
   348	  valid     : c.valid prem concl
   349	  hooks     : HooksSound P
   350	  isolation : RecordIsolated P          -- E5; trivial with no self-visible labels
   351	  inhabited : c.RegimeInhabited P facets prem  -- a run of THIS instance
   352	              -- in the claim's regime, with a fault, a sleeper where the
   353	              -- regime allows one, and an honest emission delivered
   354	  proof     : c.holds P facets prem concl
   355	
   356	structure Refutation (c : ClaimSpec) (P : ProtocolSpec n) where
   357	  facets : c.Facets P
   358	  prem   : c.Prem
   359	  concl  : c.Concl
   360	  proof  : ¬ c.holds P facets prem concl  -- by a checked run (below)
   361	```
   362	
   363	Refutation evidence:
   364	- **Kernel-feasible checking.** `native_decide` adds `Lean.ofReduceBool`,
   365	  which is not a standard axiom. Plain `decide` is too slow on real
   366	  traces (Δ = 16 ticks, times up to about 1000Δ). Refutations use a
   367	  reflective checker: a Bool function over a compact event list with one
   368	  soundness theorem per claim family (`check run = true → ¬ holds`),
   369	  written with `Nat`/`Array` so kernel reduction stays fast. Fallback:
   370	  scaled traces (Δ = 1 or 2 ticks). `native_decide` is not allowed; if
   371	  it is ever used, the matrix flags the cell.
   372	- **Finite and lasso refutations.** A bounded claim (a deadline) is
   373	  refuted by a finite run. Regimes must have a finite horizon for this
   374	  (today's regimes do). An "eventually" claim, or "no constant works"
   375	  (for example FG ping-pong without NJ), needs a lasso (periodic)
   376	  witness with a proof that the period repeats; without one it stays a
   377	  paper result and the matrix cell stays open. Each catalog entry states
   378	  which kind of refutation applies.
   379	
   380	- Theorems carry an attribute `@[cert Instance claimId]` or
   381	  `@[refutes Instance claimId]`. A Lean script reads the environment
   382	  and writes `docs/MATRIX.md` and `registry.json`: per instance and claim,
   383	  the strongest proved constants, the weakest refuted constants, or open.
   384	- A refutation at (p, c) also refutes every weaker premise and every
   385	  stronger conclusion (by `monoPrem` and `monoConcl`). "Fails the claim
   386	  for every valid parameter" is a separate, stronger refutation.
   387	- Freeze check (CI): `catalog.lock` holds, for each L1–L3 declaration, a
   388	  hash of the transitive closure of the definitions it depends on,
   389	  bodies included. A statement's type hash alone does not change when
   390	  the body of a definition it uses changes (for example a regime or
   391	  engine definition). The closure stops at L0 only because L0 is
   392	  versioned; an L0 change is a new engine version with a lift theorem.
   393	  A changed hash fails the build; a change must add a new versioned id.
   394	- The build also checks: no `sorry`, standard axioms only (as today).
   395	
   396	## 6. Kits and explorer (L5, L6)
   397	
   398	- `Kit.Engine`: protocol-agnostic lemmas over any state projection that
   399	  is monotone under `process`: honest origin of signed objects, transfer
   400	  within Δ after GST and on a healthy prefix, relay closure, clean start.
   401	  Proved once over the engine.
   402	- `Kit.Goldfish`: the shared substrate of Grid CO, SA, SA-PC and L*
   403	  (block store, vote pool with equivocation, receipt stamps, frozen
   404	  views, records). Optional for new instances.
   405	- `Kit.FG`: the fixed Simplex FG module (§7): its state, rules,
   406	  evidence, and lemmas (C)–(F) and FG-internal claims, proved once.
   407	- `Kit.Compose`: product of two specs with wiring, and the anchored
   408	  composition theorem (§7).
   409	- Explorer: each instance's functions are computable (already true).
   410	  Each catalog entry gets a decidable checker of its conclusion on a
   411	  finite run, and each regime a decidable checker of its premises
   412	  (a computable replacement for `awakeInClosed`). A found run is
   413	  emitted as a Lean file that proves a `Refutation` by `decide`.
   414	
   415	## 7. FG composition
   416	
   417	### 7.1 Decisions (user, 2026-09-28)
   418	
   419	- The FG is fixed. `Kit.FG` is our Simplex FG (the DC §7 finality gadget,
   420	  without SG, grades or safe votes) as one verified module. It is not a
   421	  catalog variable. Its lemmas (§7.4) are proved once.
   422	- Available protocols (APs) vary. An AP plugs in by proving the AP-side
   423	  entries (§7.3). The composite claims then follow from `Kit.Compose`
   424	  (§7.5) with no composite-specific proof.
   425	- FG heights never skip: a block's state height is its parent's or one
   426	  more.
   427	- Every height pair, a target and also a timeout, needs a certified
   428	  source (the AP's `FGTarget`); with no source the pair is empty. This is
   429	  a rule of the fixed module (the user's decision "timeouts need a
   430	  confirmation too; one C_fg").
   431	- History: r94 showed that a naive FG on SA breaks SA's P1. The current
   432	  FG-SA rule set uses a self-certifying source and is consistent with
   433	  this interface (Opus paper check, fgsa-fast-opus.md; Astra r143 and the
   434	  r146 adjudication are still open on parts).
   435	- Fallback: an AP that cannot meet the AP-side entries is certified
   436	  white-box as one composite instance.
   437	
   438	### 7.2 Interface
   439	
   440	**FG → AP: a fixed anchor function of a view.**
   441	
   442	`anchorsOf : View → Block × Finset Block` returns `(F, anchors)` for a
   443	block view. It is part of `Kit.FG` and does not vary.
   444	
   445	- It is evaluated at DUTY time on the duty's own view (the frozen HEAD
   446	  view, the full proposal view), not on the live store. A late witness in
   447	  the live store must not raise the band in a frozen view that does not
   448	  contain it (R1; r125 §4).
   449	- Every duty view contains the evidence basis of the current F (its
   450	  accepting carrier, its J carrier, and a descendant witness; r143 §2.1).
   451	  Without it F can be non-viable in a frozen view.
   452	- `anchors` = the viable entries at FG height h_max − 1 above the FG root:
   453	  blocks B with σ[B].h = h_max − 1 and σ[B.parent].h < h_max − 1.
   454	  Because heights never skip, each viable branch passes exactly one such
   455	  entry, so the set is a finite antichain. When the root is J
   456	  (h_max = h_j + 1), J is the entry of height h_j and anchors = {J}.
   457	  At the start, anchors = {genesis}. This set gives the same blocks as
   458	  DC's viable set (checked by the async session, round 3).
   459	- `process` does only FG admission (F ⪯ B) for the choice tree. Finality
   460	  is processed live in DC order (finality first, then height).
   461	
   462	**AP → FG: facets.**
   463	
   464	- `FGTarget`: the AP's target at the FG action, read through the base
   465	  projection. The FG uses it as the height-pair source; if it is empty,
   466	  the pair is empty.
   467	- `AnchorChoice`: the AP's rule to pick one anchor from `anchorsOf` of a
   468	  duty view. Only the HEAD and proposal walks read anchors. Other AP
   469	  rules (FG-SA's release, confirmation and lease-write gates) may run on
   470	  the unfiltered raw tree. DC's rule is the greedy walk from the root
   471	  without a majority gate below the band; FG-SA can pick the anchor that
   472	  extends its Protected lease.
   473	- FG attestations travel as block payload (the FG proposer is the block
   474	  proposer; §11 Q1).
   475	
   476	- `FinalityHead`: the AP head at the FG action. The DC finality pair
   477	  comes from it, not from `FGTarget` (r143 §3.3).
   478	- Row inclusion: honest proposals carry the FG rows they received (see
   479	  `ap.includesRows`).
   480	
   481	**Standalone form (no engine input).** AP-side entries are proved for
   482	`AP ⊗ HavocFG`. HavocFG fixes the FG state transition (heights, entries,
   483	J and F processing), admission, the anchor function and the payload
   484	format. Only the honest FG signing rule varies: it is a universally
   485	quantified parameter, and it may make honest FG signers equivocate. That
   486	is harmless for the AP claims; the FG-internal claims (accountable
   487	safety, honest never slashable) stay in `Kit.FG` with the durable Λ.
   488	`Kit.Compose` instantiates the parameter with the Simplex FG signing rule.
   489	This replaces the v3 run-input proposal (E7): a parametric composite
   490	needs no engine change.
   491	
   492	### 7.3 AP-side entries
   493	
   494	Conditions on runs of `AP ⊗ HavocFG` (L1):
   495	
   496	The conditions mention only ANCHOR VALUES at honest duties and the AP's
   497	own `FGTarget` history, never FG objects. So they are observable in
   498	`AP ⊗ HavocFG`, and AP proofs never need to know why the anchors changed.
   499	Provenance statements belong only to the FG side (§7.4 (C)).
   500	
   501	- `Static [t₀, t₁)`: at every honest duty in the interval, after the
   502	  settling time, `anchorsOf` of the duty view and F are constant, except
   503	  for changes whose new anchor set contains an anchor compatible with
   504	  every honest `FGTarget` read at or after t₀.
   505	- `TargetPreserving u`: no honest duty's anchor set excludes a block that
   506	  was an honest node's `FGTarget` at a duty at or after u.
   507	- `ProtectedCompatible` (only for the clean and Outage regimes, where the
   508	  honest Protected blocks lie on one chain): every honest anchor set
   509	  contains an anchor compatible with every honest Protected block. It is
   510	  an explicit premise of the AP claims under HavocFG: havoc honest votes
   511	  can form any quorum, so it is not implied.
   512	
   513	```text
   514	┌──────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   515	│ Entry                │ Statement                                      │
   516	├──────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   517	│ ap.floored           │ F ⪯ every AP output at every honest read       │
   518	│ ap.anchorCompliant   │ the head, the finality head and the target     │
   519	│                      │ descend the chosen anchor (needed for height   │
   520	│                      │ progress)                                      │
   521	│ ap.includesRows      │ every honest proposal includes every FG row    │
   522	│                      │ that it received before its inclusion cutoff   │
   523	│                      │ (carriers and height rise; r143 I.4, DC        │
   524	│                      │ recurrence)                                    │
   525	│ ap.honestSourced     │ an honest node's FGTarget has an honest        │
   526	│                      │ supporter that held it as Protected when it    │
   527	│                      │ signed; same-round honest targets are          │
   528	│                      │ compatible. A LOCAL fact at the node's own     │
   529	│                      │ cutoff, not a publicly checkable certificate   │
   530	│                      │ (r143 §3.1). Needed for recovery, not for FG   │
   531	│                      │ safety.                                        │
   532	│ ap.targetSafe (A)    │ under TargetPreserving u: an honest target     │
   533	│                      │ signed at or after u is in every honest store  │
   534	│                      │ by the round's action (I1); same-round honest  │
   535	│                      │ targets agree (I2); from the next round,       │
   536	│                      │ honest heads and later targets extend it (I3)  │
   537	│ ap.targetLive (B)    │ after GST, under Static for ρ: in every later  │
   538	│                      │ round of the interval the awake honest nodes   │
   539	│                      │ have one common target at the action, and it   │
   540	│                      │ advances (I4, I5 AP side). Holds even when the │
   541	│                      │ anchors exclude stale Protected blocks: the AP │
   542	│                      │ repairs by expiry or release (r143 §9.1)       │
   543	│ ap.cleanCompatible   │ in clean and Outage regimes, under             │
   544	│                      │ ProtectedCompatible: the AP's own claims hold  │
   545	└──────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   546	```
   547	
   548	The exact statements come from r147 (Astra) and blueprint-opus, which
   549	work out (S) and (L) conditional only on no FG interference, with bullets
   550	counted at a fixed height as in DC `heightProgress_pointwise`. They enter
   551	the catalog as versioned entries; the framework does not depend on their
   552	final form. `ap.targetLive` reports ρ as a conclusion constant; it may
   553	depend on participation (r146, Q5), which the premise order handles.
   554	
   555	### 7.4 FG-module lemmas (proved once in `Kit.FG`)
   556	
   557	For any AP that satisfies §7.3:
   558	
   559	- **(C) Harmless.** Every FG object with an honest row whose pair names
   560	  that node's `FGTarget` (justifications, finalizations, timeouts) does
   561	  not break `Static` or `TargetPreserving`, and keeps
   562	  `ProtectedCompatible` in the clean and Outage regimes. `Kit.Compose`
   563	  uses (C) with an explicit premise: the Simplex signing rule, with the
   564	  AP's `FGTarget` as its only source (certified pairs), makes every
   565	  honest row name the AP target or be empty. Under HavocFG this premise
   566	  is absent, so the AP-side theorems stay conditional. Outage:
   567	  before a first departing HEAD, a conflicting certified source has at
   568	  most a supporters and a < τ, so no honest row names a conflicting
   569	  block; a conflicting J needs an honest target (r143 §5.3; Opus §5).
   570	- **(D) Causal cap.** Every FG quorum has an honest row (q > a). Rows
   571	  signed before t₀ are at heights ≤ M(t₀) + 1, where M is the honest
   572	  frontier. Bullets are counted at a fixed height, as in DC
   573	  `heightProgress_pointwise`.
   574	- **(E) NJ step, two arms.** Let hF0 be the maximum h_F in the honest
   575	  stores until the frontier first reaches H + 2, where H is the first
   576	  K-multiple in [M+D+3, M+D+K+2].
   577	  - NJ arm (H − hF0 > D): nj holds at H in every honest store (the cap
   578	    gives this), no justification forms at H, the root at H + 1 is F, and
   579	    old objects can no longer change the anchors.
   580	  - Finalized arm (otherwise): start from the finalized checkpoint F*
   581	    with M < h* ≤ H + 1 (DC `HeightRegimeNamedBaseDeadline`, the
   582	    finality-cap consumer).
   583	- **(F) Certified pairs.** With the certified-source rule of §7.1, the
   584	  first H + 1 row's source is in every honest HEAD before the vote, so an
   585	  honest timeout at H + 1 cannot move the band off the AP's repair.
   586	  This replaces the v3 quiet window, which also cost latency. (r143 C12
   587	  and Opus Lemma R PROVED; the full later history is SKETCH in r143;
   588	  r146 adjudicates.)
   589	- **FG-internal.** Accountable safety, honest never slashable (needs the
   590	  durable Λ of E5/E6), finalized monotone.
   591	
   592	### 7.5 Composition theorem (`Kit.Compose`)
   593	
   594	Premises: the AP certifies §7.3; the regimes are prefix-closed; the FG
   595	liveness premises hold (FG quorums by identity: full participation or q
   596	honest awake after GST).
   597	
   598	Conclusions for AP ⊗ SimplexFG:
   599	
   600	- **Clean and Outage regimes.** With GST = 0 there are no old rows;
   601	  faulty objects alone form no quorum; by (C) honest objects keep
   602	  `ProtectedCompatible`, which discharges that premise of the AP claims.
   603	  So `ap.cleanCompatible` applies, and the AP's
   604	  safety, monotonicity, inclusion and P1 certificates carry over to the
   605	  composite (induction on time, first-departure form).
   606	- **Joint recovery.** (D) bounds the old objects by height; (E) removes
   607	  them at NJ + 1 or starts from F*; (C) and (F) keep `Static` and
   608	  `TargetPreserving` afterwards; (B) gives a common target within ρ;
   609	  (A) keeps it; the FG then progresses to finality. The bound is a
   610	  function of ρ and the FG constants (compare r143's B_NJ and Opus's
   611	  B_reset). Available recovery and joint recovery stay separate catalog
   612	  entries; the composite does not inherit the AP's θrec or recoveryFull.
   613	- **FG claims** come from `Kit.FG` directly.
   614	
   615	The FG liveness premises (by identity) differ from the AP's sleepy
   616	premises. The joint recovery claim states both.
