   256	## 4. Claim catalog (L3)
   257	
   258	```lean
   259	structure ClaimSpec where
   260	  id       : Lean.Name                 -- immutable, e.g. `ac.safe.v1`
   261	  Facets   : {n : Nat} → ProtocolSpec n → Type
   262	  Prem     : Type                      -- premise parameters (θrec, faultCap,
   263	                                       -- recoveryFull, Π, q, …)
   264	  Concl    : Type                      -- conclusion parameters (D, ρ, A, …)
   265	  valid    : Prem → Concl → Prop
   266	  weakerPrem   : Prem → Prem → Prop    -- preorder: p ≤ p' = p admits more runs
   267	  strongerConcl : Concl → Concl → Prop -- preorder: c ≤ c' = c promises more
   268	  holds    : {V : Type} → [Fintype V] → [DecidableEq V] →
   269	             {n : Nat} → (P : ProtocolSpec n) → Facets P → Prem → Concl → Prop
   270	  monoPrem  : weakerPrem p p' → holds P F p c → holds P F p' c
   271	  monoConcl : strongerConcl c c' → holds P F p c → holds P F p c'
   272	```
   273	
   274	The user's rule is that tradeoffs are reported constants. Premise
   275	parameters (SA recovery at h>a versus SA-PC at a≤f with q ≤ awake
   276	honest; θrec; recoveryFull; faultCap; Π) are ordered opposite to
   277	conclusion parameters: a weaker premise is a stronger claim. With one
   278	order for both, dominance would be wrong.
   279	
   280	- `holds` quantifies over every environment and run of its regime.
   281	  Regimes come only from L1; an instance cannot add a premise.
   282	- Every regime is prefix-closed or states that it is not. The anchored
   283	  composition (§7) needs prefix-closed regimes.
   284	- Constants belong to one claim. A cross-claim bound (`Π ≤ η`,
   285	  `A_f ≤ A`) goes into a claim that reads both constants.
   286	- `monoPrem` and `monoConcl` are proved once per claim. With them,
   287	  dominance is computable: instance X dominates Y on a claim set S when
   288	  X certifies every claim of S with premises at least as weak and
   289	  conclusions at least as strong.
   290	- Relation lemmas (`implies`, `equivalent`) between catalog entries are
   291	  proved once. Derived certificates follow without instance work.
   292	
   293	Every catalog entry also needs:
   294	- a discriminating witness: a trivial instance from the cheater set
   295	  (`alwaysGenesis`, echo-proposal, and so on) that fails it. This shows
   296	  that the claim is not vacuous;
   297	- a regime witness at catalog level, as today (K8).
   298	
   299	**Profiles.** A profile is a named set of catalog ids. `ac-4.3` is the
   300	current bundle. A proved equivalence `AvailableChain ↔ ac-4.3` feeds
   301	today's theorems (`gridContract_from_engine`, `saContract_from_engine`,
   302	`sapcContract_from_engine`) into the matrix unchanged. `StrongClaims`
   303	becomes profile `strong-1` the same way. The user decides which profile
   304	is the required floor for a search task.
   305	
   306	`RecoveredAt` is split: each Sleepy claim gets a recovered variant
   307	(`ac.recovered.safe.v1`, …), because FG-SA joint recovery needs them
   308	separately (async review, Q5). `ac-4.3` contains all four, so the
