   114	def NoFutureRead (P : AvailableChain.Generic.ProtocolSpec n)
   115	    (E : AvailableChain.Generic.Env V) (I : Interface P)
   116	    (rho : AvailableChain.Generic.Run V P) (g : Output P) : Prop :=
   117	  ∀ v ∈ rho.honest, ∀ t (s : Slot) (B : Block),
   118	    HonestProposalAt E I rho s B → t < P.proposalTime s →
   119	    ¬ Block.Preceq B (readAt P rho g v t)
   120	
   121	end AvailableChain.Statements.Generic
   122	
   123	end
     1	module
     2	public import AvailableChainModel.Generic.Run
     3	public import AvailableChainModel.Objects.Blocks
     4	
     5	@[expose] public section
     6	
     7	namespace AvailableChain.Statements.Generic
     8	
     9	open AvailableChain
    10	
    11	variable {V : Type} [DecidableEq V] {n : Nat}
    12	
    13	/-- What an instance exposes. The proposal schedule (`proposalTime`,
    14	`slotsAt`) is part of the protocol; the proposer of a slot is run data
    15	(`Run.proposerOf`). There is no committee field: every label has a committee of
    16	all `n` seats, assigned by the run. -/
    17	structure Interface (P : AvailableChain.Generic.ProtocolSpec n) where
    18	  /-- The two user-facing outputs, as getters on the node state. -/
    19	  confirmed : P.State → Block
    20	  stable : P.State → Block
    21	  /-- The block that a proposal payload proposes. -/
    22	  block : P.Payload → Option Block
    23	  /-- Slots at which a period opens. This is used only by the recurrence
    24	  premise. -/
    25	  opening : Slot → Prop
    26	
    27	abbrev Output {n : Nat} (P : AvailableChain.Generic.ProtocolSpec n) :=
    28	  P.State → Block
    29	
    30	/-- The output `g` read at node `v` and time `t`. -/
    31	def readAt (P : AvailableChain.Generic.ProtocolSpec n)
    32	    (rho : AvailableChain.Generic.Run V P) (g : Output P)
    33	    (v : V) (t : Time) : Block :=
    34	  g (AvailableChain.Generic.Run.readAt P rho t v)
    35	
    36	end AvailableChain.Statements.Generic
    37	
    38	end
     1	# available-chain — contract draft
     2	
     3	_Draft 4.3 (2026-09-27), committee model C*. Purpose: a small Lean repo
     4	whose engine and claims are fixed, so that any suitable protocol can be an instance if it proves the
     5	claims. Source of the engine: verified-consensus
     6	`DecoupledConsensusModel/Generic` (Env, ProtocolSpec, Run) and
     7	`DecoupledConsensusStatements/Generic`._
     8	
     9	## 1. Decisions
    10	
    11	```text
    12	┌────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    13	│ Topic                  │ Decision                                        │
    14	├────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
    15	│ Sleep                  │ Asleep nodes receive and process, but do not    │
    16	│                        │ tick (no emission). Join rule = "listen for a   │
    17	│                        │ while"; no state transfer is modelled.          │
    18	│ Committees             │ Model C* (mean-field seats): every label has a  │
    19	│                        │ committee of all n seats; the run assigns seats │
    20	│                        │ to validators by a fresh hidden bijection per   │
    21	│                        │ label. Claims hold for every assignment. This   │
    22	│                        │ is the exact form of "perfectly representative" │
    23	│                        │ (no sampling error). See §2a.                   │
    24	│ Weights                │ Unit seats: every validator has weight 1.       │
    25	│ Corruption             │ Static: the faulty set is fixed for the run and │
    26	│                        │ its size is the regime's threshold. No adaptive │
    27	│                        │ corruption.                                     │
    28	│ Scope                  │ Outputs stable and confirmed only. No FG, no SG.│
    29	│ Genericity             │ Generic engine and outputs. Slots, proposers,   │
    30	│                        │ proposal times, duty labels and blocks stay in  │
    31	│                        │ the model (not 100% generic, by choice).        │
    32	│ Liveness               │ Through honest-proposal inclusion, as in        │
    33	│                        │ verified-consensus.                             │
    34	│ Sleepy start           │ GST = 0 and t₀ = 0. A later GST needs recovery. │
    35	│ Recovery              │ An instance sets θrec and recoveryFull.         │
    36	└────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
    37	```
    38	
    39	Tradeoffs go into constants, not claims. Every design must be able to
    40	meet every claim trivially with some constant. An instance can set its
    41	fast arming lag equal to its general arming lag and use general P1 for
    42	fast P1. A smaller fast lag expresses a stronger guarantee.
    43	
    44	## 2. Engine (fixed)
    45	
    46	- **Env:** Δ, t_GST, `awake : V → Time → Bool`. No weights (unit seats).
    47	- **ProtocolSpec n:** `State`, `Label`, `Payload`, `init`, `clock`,
    48	  `actTime`, `duties`, `act`, `proposalTime`, `slotsAt`, `propose`,
    49	  `gossip`, `process`, and the delivery hooks (`wellFormed`, `depsPresent`,
    50	  `processed`, `excludes`, `handles`, `carries`). Wire objects are
    51	  `Signed Label n Payload` (authentication and payload). The type does not
    52	  mention validators.
    53	- **Run:** honest set (static corruption), horizon, event list (ticks,
    54	  deliveries), `seatOf : Label → Fin n ≃ V`, `proposerOf : Slot → V`.
    55	- **Step:** a tick runs `clock`; a delivery runs `process`. Emission never
    56	  changes the state. At a tick, an awake node emits: for each label due now,
    57	  `act st ℓ` signed with its seat of ℓ; for each of its slots due now,
    58	  `propose st s` signed with `proposer s`; and unsigned `gossip st t`. An
    59	  asleep node emits nothing but still receives, processes, and runs its
    60	  clock.
    61	- **Signatures:** an object held by an honest seat or proposer was emitted
    62	  by it. Faulty seats and proposers sign anything at any time.
    63	- **Delivery:** arbitrary before GST; after GST every honest emission
    64	  reaches every honest node in less than Δ. Accepted objects also reach
    65	  acceptance at every honest reader within Δ when the deadline is in the
    66	  run. A healthy outage prefix has the same acceptance guarantee before b₀.
    67	
    68	## 2a. Committee model C* (rules)
    69	
    70	```text
    71	┌────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┐
    72	│ K  │ Rule                                       │ Where                 │
    73	├────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
    74	│ K1 │ Seat authentication per label, with a      │ Auth, Run.seatOf      │
    75	│    │ fresh run-chosen bijection per label       │                       │
    76	│ K2 │ No identity in honest code: no V, one      │ ProtocolSpec n        │
    77	│    │ init, no keys; the engine signs            │                       │
    78	│ K3 │ Emission never changes state; own objects  │ World.step            │
    79	│    │ return only by delivery                    │                       │
    80	│ K4 │ Unit seats                                 │ Env (no weights)      │
    81	│ K5 │ Honest seat signs once, at actTime ℓ, if   │ duties_spec,          │
    82	│    │ awake; faulty seats unconstrained          │ Run.emittedAt         │
    83	│ K6 │ Committee = all n seats (absolute vs       │ Run.seatOf            │
    84	│    │ relative thresholds use awake)             │                       │
    85	│ K7 │ Implementation obligation: a fresh private │ not checkable in Lean │
    86	│    │ VRF per label; a seat signs at one instant │                       │
    87	│    │ only. Bundle same-instant duties in one    │                       │
    88	│    │ label, or use separate labels.             │                       │
    89	│ K8 │ Witnesses with faults, a sleeper, and two  │ Witnesses             │
    90	│    │ distinct honest views; the accumulation    │                       │
    91	│    │ attack must be a valid run                 │                       │
    92	│ K9 │ Optional slack ε (finite committees)       │ later                 │
    93	│ K10│ Disclosures: no corruption of revealed     │ this document         │
    94	│    │ committees; joiners receive full history   │                       │
    95	└────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘
    96	```
    97	
    98	Why RLMD-GHOST is not an instance: no signature links votes of different
    99	labels, so "latest message per validator" cannot be written. Links in
   100	payloads are unauthenticated and faulty seats can forge them. K3 removes the
   101	one gap of C* against real committees: a node cannot learn or use its own
   102	seats, so it uses only what an unsampled node can compute.
   103	
   104	## 3. Interface (what an instance exposes)
   105	
   106	- `confirmed, stable : P.State → Block`;
   107	- `block : P.Payload → Option Block` (the block a proposal proposes);
   108	- `opening : Slot → Prop` for the recurrence premise.
   109	
   110	The proposal schedule is in the protocol (`proposalTime`, `slotsAt`); the
   111	proposer is run data. `Block` has no proposer identity. Removed: `committee`,
   112	`proposer`, `proposedBlock`, `finalized`, and all certificate, evidence and
   113	slashing fields.
   114	
   115	## 4. Regimes (fixed menu; instances cannot add premises)
   116	
   117	Notation at time t: H_t = honest validators awake at t; H_[t−η,t] = honest
   118	validators awake at some time in [t−η, t]; A = faulty validators (static,
   119	always counted). Weights are counts (unit seats). Under C* every committee
   120	has the fractions of the whole set, so the premises are stated on the whole
   121	set.
   122	
   123	All regimes require `RootInjective E I rho`. This is the collision-resistance
   124	premise for block roots. Its scope contains genesis and every ancestor
   125	(including the tip itself) of a block returned by `I.block` on a delivered
   126	or emitted payload. Within this scope, equal roots imply equal blocks.
   127	Sleepy, Outage, and Recovery use the same predicate. StableSleepy and Fast
   128	inherit it from Sleepy. This does not restrict the raw `Block` datatype.
   129	
   130	Outage participation premises hold at every `0 ≤ t < b₀`, including
   131	times before the protected read `T`. The healthy prefix starts at genesis.
   132	
   133	**η-sleepy condition with threshold θ** (TSQ type, decision A):
   134	
   135	    w(H_t)  >  θ · w(H_[t−η,t] ∪ A)        for every t in the regime
   136	
   137	- θ = 1/2 gives w(H_t) > w(A) + w(dropped), where dropped = H_[t−η,t] \ H_t:
   138	  the honest validators awake now outweigh the faulty ones plus the honest
   139	  ones that went to sleep in the last η (the FreshMajority shape).
   140	- The instance declares θ in [1/2, 3/4]. θ = 1/2 gives the strongest
   141	  instance claim: its threshold premise holds for the most runs. η is an
   142	  instance parameter. The contract requires Π ≤ η.
   143	
   144	```text
   145	┌──────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   146	│ Regime       │ Premise                                               │
   147	├──────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   148	│ Sleepy       │ GST = t₀ = 0, synchrony and accepted-object relay;    │
   149	│              │ at every t, w(H_t) > w(A)                             │
   150	│ StableSleepy │ Sleepy, and the η-sleepy condition with threshold θ   │
   151	│ (θ, η)       │ (for the stable claims)                               │
   152	│ Fast         │ Sleepy with full honest participation and w(A) < W/4  │
   153	│              │ (honest > 3/4). Decided by the user.                  │
   154	│ Outage       │ StableSleepy(θ, η) counts before b₀; healthy delivery │
   155	│              │ and accepted relay before b₀; arbitrary delivery in   │
   156	│              │ [b₀, b₁)                                              │
   157	│ FastOutage   │ Outage with the fast lag instead of the general lag;  │
   158	│              │ at least ⌈n/2⌉ honest awake at every 0 ≤ t < b₀       │
   159	│ Recovery     │ arbitrary before GST; after GST, θrec·(h_t+a)<h_t;    │
   160	│              │ full participation if recoveryFull; honest awake      │
   161	│              │ opening proposer every G                              │
   162	└──────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   163	```
   164	
   165	## 5. Claims (the bundle every instance proves)
   166	
   167	```text
   168	┌─────────────────────┬──────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐
   169	│ Claim               │ Regime   │ Conclusion                               │
   170	├─────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
   171	│ confirmedSafe       │ Sleepy   │ honest confirmed reads are compatible    │
   172	│ stableSafe          │ Sleepy*  │ honest stable reads are compatible       │
   173	│ monotone            │ Sleepy   │ no honest read of either output retreats │
   174	│ nested              │ always   │ stable ⪯ confirmed at every honest read  │
   175	│ confirmedIncluded   │ Sleepy   │ an honest proposer awake at slot s       │
   176	│                     │          │ proposes, and its block is in every      │
   177	│                     │          │ honest confirmed read by                 │
   178	│                     │          │ proposalTime s + D_c                     │
   179	│ stableIncluded      │ StableS. │ same for stable, by + D_s                │
   180	│ stableIncludedFast  │ Fast     │ same, by + D_f (target: small)           │
   181	│ stableResilient     │ Outage   │ a stable read at T persists through      │
   182	│                     │          │ [b₀, b₁) if b₀ ≥ T + A and b₁ − b₀ ≤ Π   │
   183	│ stableResilientFast │ FastOut. │ the same persistence with fast lag A_f   │
   184	│                     │          │ and the full-prefix high count premise   │
   185	│ recovered           │ Recovery │ the Sleepy claims hold from GST + ρ      │
   186	└─────────────────────┴──────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘
   187	```
   188	
   189	`Sleepy*` means the plain Sleepy regime for safety. The stable inclusion
   190	claim uses StableSleepy(θ, η).
   191	
   192	`ExecutionValid` implies that every event has nonnegative time. Thus each
   193	state before time zero is `P.init`. Sleepy needs no separate clean-start
   194	field. An outage can start after a healthy prefix. Recovery can start from
   195	an arbitrary reachable state before GST.
   196	
   197	`nested` is cheap (an accessor can enforce it); its value is only with the
   198	two safety claims.
   199	
   200	## 6. Constants (the instance's output)
   201	
   202	θ and θrec in [1/2, 3/4], recoveryFull, η, D_c, D_s, D_f, arming lags
   203	A and A_f with 0 ≤ A_f ≤ A, budget Π ≤ η, recovery bound ρ, and
   204	the Recovery premise parameter G (gap between honest awake opening
   205	proposers; ρ may depend on G). All in units of Δ and regime parameters; none may depend on the run.
   206	Protocols are compared by these constants.
   207	
   208	## 7. Non-vacuity
   209	
   210	- A witness execution for each regime (in `AvailableChainWitnesses`).
   211	- An accepted-object witness with an active cross-reader relay deadline.
   212	- A sanity instance ("always output genesis") that proves the safety claims
   213	  and provably fails `confirmedIncluded`.
   214	
   215	## 8. Open choices
   216	
   217	1. DECIDED: local repo at ~/ethereum/lean/available-chain (no remote).
   218	   Toolchain leanprover/lean4:v4.30.0-rc2 with Mathlib v4.30.0-rc2, as in
   219	   verified-consensus and fast-confirmation: the engine ports without API
   220	   drift, and the local Mathlib cache is reused.
   221	2. DECIDED: fast regime = full participation and honest > 3/4.
   222	3. DECIDED: participation drop in a separate StableSleepy(θ, η) regime
   223	   (decision A). Each instance declares θ.
   224	4. Time: keep verified-consensus `Time`.
   225	
   226	## Draft 4 decisions and reasons
   227	
   228	- Sleepy starts at time zero with GST zero. When GST is later, recovery is
   229	  necessary. The engine and `ExecutionValid` prove the clean initial state.
   230	- Each instance sets θ. This keeps the η-sleepy premise explicit and lets
   231	  the instance state the strength that its proof supports.
   232	- Recovery uses a current-count threshold θrec and an optional full
   233	  participation flag. This permits healing after an arbitrary prefix and
   234	  permits an instance to use relative-half participation.
   235	- Accepted-object relay is a regime field. A handler call alone can reject
   236	  an object, so it cannot establish cross-reader acceptance.
   237	- There is one `AvailableChain` bundle. Grid CO uses θrec = 1/2 and
   238	  recoveryFull = false. L* uses θrec = 3/4 and recoveryFull = true.
   239	- Grid CO declares θ_R and proves that its grid level exceeds each faulty
   240	  count `a ≥ 1` when `h ≤ n` and `θ_R·(h+a) < h`. The bound `h ≤ n` holds
   241	  for an awake count in the n-validator model.
   242	
   243	## Draft 4.1 change
   244	
   245	The outage fields `sleepyBefore` and `etaSleepyBefore` now require their
   246	count conditions from time zero, not from the protected read time `T`.
   247	This applies the standing decision that Sleepy-family premises start at
   248	genesis. It excludes the Draft 4 pending-gate counterexample described in
   249	`GRIDCO-STATUS.md`. No other contract field or claim changed.
   250	
   251	## Draft 4.2 change
   252	
   253	The shared `RootInjective E I rho` premise makes collision resistance of
   254	block roots explicit. It covers genesis, delivered and emitted proposal
   255	blocks, and all their ancestors. This is the scope needed by the reference
   256	`NamedRootCollisionFree` and `RootInjectiveBelow` proofs. The round-14
   257	root-collision run does not meet this premise. See `GRIDCO-STATUS.md`,
   258	“Resolved in Draft 4.2”. No claim or constant changed.
   259	
   260	## Draft 4.3 change
   261	
   262	Fast P1 is now the `stableResilientFast` field of `AvailableChain`.
   263	`Constants.fastArmingLag` satisfies `0 ≤ fastArmingLag ≤ armingLag`.
   264	`FastOutageRegime` extends `OutageRegime` with a constants copy that
   265	sets only `armingLag := fastArmingLag`, and adds
   266	`∀ t, 0 ≤ t → t < b₀ → (n+1)/2 ≤ (awakeAt E rho.honest t).card`.
   267	Integer division gives `⌈n/2⌉`. All other outage premises and the
   268	persistence conclusion are retained. The general lag is not an extra test.
   269	
   270	SA and SA-PC set the fast lag to `4Δ`. Grid CO and L* set it equal to
   271	their general lag. Grid CO derives the new field from general P1; L*
   272	has the same conditional derivation, with its claim proofs still pending.
   273	SA uses `fastP1_from_engine` in its complete bundle. SA-PC keeps the
   274	proof pending with its other unfinished claims. The separate SA and
   275	SA-PC extra-claim statements are removed.
   276	
   277	This is the one-time authorized scope extension. The base contract is
   278	now frozen at Draft 4.3. The strong companion below is additive.
   279	
   280	## Strong companion (additive)
   281	
   282	The optional companion in `AvailableChainStatements/Strong/` adds a
   283	`StrongInterface` with a `strong : State → Block` output and
   284	`StrongConstants` with a fault cap and inclusion delay. It changes no
   285	existing definition, regime, constant, or field of `AvailableChain`.
   286	There is no unconditional order between `strong` and the base outputs.
   287	
   288	`StrongClaims` requires safety (agreement and monotonicity) under Sleepy
   289	and the cap; inclusion under Sleepy, full honest participation, and the
   290	cap; persistence after sound arming; and safety and inclusion after
   291	Recovery, with the cap explicit. SA-PC sets the cap to `⌊(n−1)/3⌋` and
   292	the strong inclusion delay to `6Δ`.
   293	
   294	`StrongArmedRegime` requires execution validity, root injectivity,
   295	healthy delivery and accepted-object relay from genesis before `b₀`,
   296	`h_t > a` at every `0 ≤ t < b₀`, the cap, and `T + Δ ≤ b₀`.
   297	It has no certificate-possession premise, η-sleepy premise, outage
   298	budget, or delivery or participation premise after `b₀`. The instance
   299	must prove sound arming from these fields. Persistence covers all
   300	times from `b₀` through the run horizon, with no bound on that horizon.
   301	
   302	The base bundle retains its original fault scopes. In particular,
   303	SA-PC's base clean and outage claims have no added cap. Its unchanged
   304	Recovery regime, with `θrec = 2/3` and `recoveryFull = true`, implies
   305	the cap; the instance must prove that implication.

